------------------------------------- Bet on a pony --------------------------------------------------


- BET ON A PONY FROM A RACE DETAIL PAGE USING THE ROUTER, INPUTS, AND RESOURCES.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Router" chapter

- Now that we can call the secure API(from chapter 17 exercise - HTTP with authentication), we
    should be able to bet on ponies.

- Betting on a pony, in a race, consists in sending a POST request
    (see https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com/apidoc#resources-bet-create).


1️⃣ 👉Let’s create a new method bet(raceId: number, ponyId: number): Observable<void> in RaceService,
    which of course uses HttpClient to call our API.
    👉Let’s also create a method get(raceId: () => number): ResourceRef<RaceModel | undefined>,
    allowing to get the detail of a race by its ID. 🧠The raceId parameter must be a function,
    because it allows to use this method in a component where the ID of the race is a signal
    (get(raceId())) or a simple number (get(() => raceId)).
    race-service.ts:

        bet(raceId: number, ponyId: number): Observable<void> {
            return this.http.post<void>(`${environment.baseUrl}/api/races/${raceId}/bets`, { ponyId });
        }

        get(raceId: () => number): ResourceRef<RaceModel | undefined> {
            return httpResource<RaceModel>(() => `${environment.baseUrl}/api/races/${raceId()}`);
        }


2️⃣ Then let’s create a new component Bet to deal with the bets in a race. We would like this
    component to display for a URL like /races/123, where 123 is the ID of the race. It means that
    🧠we’ll have a route '/races' which displays the list of races, and a 🧠route '/races/123' which
    displays the race detail and allows to bet on it. When a configuration like this one presents
    itself, we can use the children attribute in the route configuration:

        {
            path: 'users',
            children: [
                { path: '', component: Users },
                { path: ':userId', component: User }
            ]
        }
    
    🔥This kind of configuration then allows to apply security "guards" to all the children. It also
    allows to simplify the lazy-loading configuration. That’s what we’ll do in a more advanced
    exercise! Do the same as the example with Races and Bet in the route configuration, and 🧠name
    the dynamic parameter raceId.
    app.routes.ts:

        {
            path: 'races',
            children: [
                { path: '', component: Races },
                { path: ':raceId', component: Bet }
            ]
        }


